# NOETHERVIM: WRITING AND CODE PROFILES

Open a `.tex` file and a `.py` file and they behave differently before you
touch anything. That is not the plugins disagreeing; it is one deliberate
split. This lesson explains which buffer gets which treatment, and how to
disagree with it per window.

Table of contents:

- [Two profiles](*profiles*)
- [What the writing profile does](*writing*)
- [What the code profile does](*code*)
- [Buffers in neither](*neither*)
- [Disagreeing, per window](*toggles*)
- [Spelling](*spell*)
- [Changing the lists](*config*)
- [Recap](*recap*)

## TWO PROFILES *profiles*

Prose and code want opposite things from the same options. A line break in
prose is a rendering detail; in code it is syntax. So NoetherVim sorts every
filetype into one of three groups and sets a handful of window options to
match.

    writing    tex, markdown, norg, text, gitcommit, mail, rst, typst
    code       every filetype that is not in the other two groups
    neither    json, yaml, toml, help, man, quickfix, oil, terminal,
             and the plugin panels

The full statement of what each sets lives in
[the filetype profiles](noethervim-filetype-profiles).

## WHAT THE WRITING PROFILE DOES *writing*

    `wrap` and `linebreak`   long lines fold at the window edge, at spaces
    `spell`                  spellcheck on
    `conceallevel = 2`       markup renders rather than showing its source
    `formatoptions` plus `t`   typing past the text width breaks the line

Wrapped lines are marked. A continuation row shows `↳` in the number
column, so a line that ran past the edge is never mistaken for a line of its
own. It costs no text cell, so continuation rows stay lined up under the
text they continue. See [the wrap marker](noethervim-wrap-marker).

That last option is the one to know about: the writing profile **hard
wraps**, inserting a real line break at the text width as you type. That is
what you want for prose in version control, and it has one consequence. A
phrase can end up split across two lines, and `/brown fox` then finds
nothing. The `wrapsearch` bundle makes `/` and `?` match across the break.
See [hard wrapping](noethervim-hard-wrap).

## WHAT THE CODE PROFILE DOES *code*

    `list`     tabs and trailing spaces are drawn

Long lines run off the edge rather than folding, since `wrap` is off
everywhere but prose. A line running past the window is marked `›` at the edge,
and `‹` on the other side when it continues back. Vim draws those only while
`list` is on, which is why they arrive together.

`formatoptions` omits `t` globally, so typing past the text width in code
does not break the line. Formatters handle line length instead.

## BUFFERS IN NEITHER *neither*

Structured text and plugin panels are left alone on purpose. A JSON file is
not prose, but drawing whitespace in it is noise; a terminal has its own
ideas about wrapping. These keep whatever their own plugin or ftplugin set.

This tutorial is one of them, which is why it is not showing you trailing
whitespace while you read it.

## DISAGREEING, PER BUFFER *toggles*

The profile is a default, not a ruling. Every option it sets has a toggle
pair, and a toggle is yours: it holds for that buffer, in every window
showing it, while other buffers keep the profile.

    `[ow` `]ow`   wrap on / off
    `[os` `]os`   spell on / off
    `[ol` `]ol`   whitespace characters on / off
    `[on` `]on`   line numbers on / off
    `[oc` `]oc`   cursorline on / off
    `[ot` `]ot`   the color column on / off

Try it here. Press `[ow` and the long paragraphs in this lesson start
folding at the window edge, with `↳` marking each continuation row. Press
`]ow` to put it back.

The same goes for a setting in your own files: a `vim.opt_local.spell =
false` in `after/ftplugin/markdown.lua` holds in every markdown buffer, and
`vim.o.spell = false` in your options file turns spelling off in prose too.
Prose still wraps whatever your global `wrap` says; set it per filetype.

Fill in the pair member that turns wrapping on:

Wrap on, for this buffer: ___

## SPELLING *spell*

Spellcheck is on in writing buffers and off in code, and three keys make it
usable rather than a source of underlines.

    `<C-l>`   insert mode: fix the nearest misspelling behind the cursor
            and carry on typing, leaving the cursor where it was
    `zg`      add the word under the cursor to your dictionary
    `zG`      the same, for this session only

`zg` also adds the possessive form, which Vim does not derive on its own for
a lowercase entry and which you would otherwise come back for. It leaves the
lowercase form of a capitalized word alone, so a proper noun is still
flagged in lowercase. See [adding a word](noethervim-spell-add).

To spellcheck code comments too, set `spell_in_code` in your config; it
scopes spelling to comments and strings rather than identifiers.

## CHANGING THE LISTS *config*

If a filetype is in the wrong group, move it rather than fighting the
options. In `lua/user/config.lua`, `profiles = { classes = { tex = false } }`
leaves one filetype alone, and `profiles = false` turns both off. The lists:

~~~ cmd
    :NoetherVim templates
~~~

    `writing_filetypes`    extra filetypes that count as prose
    `non_code_filetypes`   extra filetypes that skip both profiles
    `spell_in_code`        spellcheck comments and strings in code

All three are additive; the defaults stay.
[Lesson five](@tutor:noethervim-05-your-config) covers writing that file.

There is also a statusline marker for this, off by default. `<C-w>sf` turns
it on: a pencil for writing, angle brackets for code, nothing for a buffer
in neither. Clicking it reports the live value of every option the profile
set, so your toggles show up too.

